BT 1 0 0 1 0 86 Tm /F1 10 Tf 12 TL 1.052847 Tw (A few years ago, I tried to port the Lua scripting language to STM32F4 microcontroller, and then I experienced a) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.241853 Tw (Lua-based firmware on ESP8266: NodeMCU. These experiences made me experience the convenience of script) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.667606 Tw (development. Later, I came into contact with some scripting languages, such as Python, JavaScript, Basic, and) Tj T* 0 Tw -0.123106 Tw (MATLAB. At present, only a few languages are suitable for transplanting to the microcontroller platform. I used to pay) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.783373 Tw (more attention to Lua because it is a very compact embedded scripting language, and its design goal is to be) Tj T* 0 Tw .124116 Tw (embedded in the host program. However, for the microcontroller, the Lua interpreter may not be small enough, and I) Tj T* 0 Tw .557338 Tw (cannot run it on a 32-bit microcontroller with a relatively small memory. To this end, I started to read Lua code and) Tj T* 0 Tw (developed my own scripting language-Berry on this basis.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 40.01575 571.8394 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 50 Tm /F1 10 Tf 12 TL 1.23315 Tw (This is an ultra-lightweight embedded scripting language, it is also a multi-paradigm dynamic language. Supports) Tj T* 0 Tw -0.11106 Tw (object-oriented, process-oriented and functional \(less support\) styles. Many aspects of this language refer to Lua, but) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.765535 Tw (its syntax also borrows from the design of other languages. If the reader already knows a high-level language,) Tj T* 0 Tw .616005 Tw (Berry\222s grammar should be very easy to grasp: the language has only some simple rules and a very natural scope) Tj T* 0 Tw (design.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 40.01575 409.8394 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 146 Tm /F1 10 Tf 12 TL .004006 Tw (The main application scenario I consider is embedded systems with low performance. The memory of these systems) Tj T* 0 Tw .92829 Tw (may be very small, so it is very difficult to run a full-featured scripting language. This means that we may have to) Tj T* 0 Tw .296711 Tw (make a choice. Berry may only provide the most commonly used and basic core functions, while other unnecessary) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.340829 Tw (features are only used as optional modules. This will inevitably lead to the language\222s standard library being too) Tj T* 0 Tw .105256 Tw (small, even the language There will also be uncertain designs \(such as the implementation of floating-point numbers) Tj T* 0 Tw 2.189006 Tw (and integers, etc.\). The benefits of these trade-offs are more room for optimization, while the disadvantage is) Tj T* 0 Tw -0.078279 Tw (obviously the inconsistency of language standards. Berry\222s interpreter refers to the implementation of Lua interpreter,) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.6073 Tw (which is mainly divided into two parts: compiler and virtual machine. Berry\222s compiler is a one-pass compiler to) Tj T* 0 Tw -0.065053 Tw (generate bytecode. This solution does not generate an abstract syntax tree, so it saves memory. The virtual machine) Tj T* 0 Tw .804672 Tw (is a register type. Generally speaking, the register type virtual machine is more efficient than the stack type virtual) Tj T* 0 Tw .277131 Tw (machine. In addition to implementing language features, we also hope to optimize the memory usage and operating) Tj T* 0 Tw -0.130347 Tw (efficiency of the interpreter. At present, the performance indicators of Berry interpreter are not comparable to those of) Tj T* 0 Tw (mainstream languages, but the memory footprint is very small.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 40.01575 343.8394 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 50 Tm /F1 10 Tf 12 TL 2.675256 Tw (It wasn\222t until later that I learned about the MicroPython project: a simplified Python interpreter designed for) Tj T* 0 Tw .041606 Tw (microcontrollers. Nowadays, Python is very hot, and this Python interpreter designed for microcontrollers is also very) Tj T* 0 Tw .752594 Tw (popular. Compared with the current mature technology, Berry is a new language without a sufficient user base. Its) Tj T* 0 Tw .176005 Tw (advantage is that it is easy to master the grammar and may have advantages in terms of resource consumption and) Tj T* 0 Tw (performance.) Tj T* ET
Q
Q
q
1 0 0 1 40.01575 253.8394 cm
q
0 0 0 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 0 74 Tm /F1 10 Tf 12 TL .395909 Tw (If you need to port the Berry interpreter, you need to ensure that the compiler you use provides support for the C99) Tj T* 0 Tw .404672 Tw (standard \(I previously fully complied with C89, and some optimization work later made me give up this decision\). At) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.290881 Tw (present, most compilers provide support for C99, and the common compilers such as ARMCC \(KEIL MDK\), ICC) Tj T* 0 Tw 2.671606 Tw (\(IAR\) and GCC in the development of ARM processors also support C99. This document introduces Berry\222s) Tj T* 0 Tw .766506 Tw (grammar rules, standard library and other facilities, and finally guides readers to transplant and extend Berry. This) Tj T* 0 Tw 2.593606 Tw (document does not explain the implementation mechanism of the interpreter, and may be explained in other) Tj T* 0 Tw (documents if we have time.) Tj T* ET
