BT 1 0 0 1 0 62 Tm /F1 10 Tf 12 TL 1.025479 Tw (A string is a sequence of characters. In terms of storage, Berry divides strings into long strings and short strings.) Tj T* 0 Tw .196705 Tw (There is only one instance of the same short character string in memory, and all short character strings are linked in) Tj T* 0 Tw .687888 Tw (a hash table. This design helps to improve the performance of string equality comparison and can reduce memory) Tj T* 0 Tw .209719 Tw (usage. Since the use frequency of long strings is low, and the overhead of hash operation is quite high, they are not) Tj T* 0 Tw .107338 Tw (linked to the hash table, so there may be multiple identical instances in the memory. The string is read-only after it is) Tj T* 0 Tw (created. Therefore, \223modifying\224 the string will generate a new string, and the original string will not be modified.) Tj T* ET
