BT 1 0 0 1 0 50 Tm .206005 Tw 12 TL /F1 10 Tf 0 0 0 rg (Berry does not care about the format or encoding of characters. For example, the string ) Tj /F3 10 Tf (\222abc\222) Tj /F1 10 Tf ( is actually the ASCII) Tj T* 0 Tw -0.115295 Tw (code of the characters ) Tj /F3 10 Tf (\222a\222) Tj /F1 10 Tf (, ) Tj /F3 10 Tf (\222b\222) Tj /F1 10 Tf ( and ) Tj /F3 10 Tf (\222c\222) Tj /F1 10 Tf (. Therefore, if there are wide characters in the string \(the character length is) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.029163 Tw (greater than 1 byte\), the number of characters in the string cannot be directly counted. In fact, using the ) Tj /F3 10 Tf (size\(\)) Tj /F1 10 Tf  T* 0 Tw 2.000205 Tw (function can only get the number of bytes in the string. In addition, in order to facilitate interaction with the C) Tj T* 0 Tw (language, Berry\222s string always ends with ) Tj /F3 10 Tf (\222\\0\222) Tj /F1 10 Tf ( characters. This feature is transparent to the Berry program.) Tj T* ET
