BT 1 0 0 1 0 98 Tm .379689 Tw 12 TL /F1 10 Tf 0 0 0 rg (As mentioned earlier, functions are the first type of value in Berry. You can define functions anywhere, and you can) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.7973 Tw (also pass functions as parameters or return values. When another function is defined in a function, the nested) Tj T* 0 Tw -0.138795 Tw (function can access the local variables of any outer function. We call the \223local variables of the outer function\224 used in) Tj T* 0 Tw .456711 Tw (the function the function ) Tj /F2 10 Tf (Free variable) Tj /F1 10 Tf (. The generalized free variables also include global variables, but there is no) Tj T* 0 Tw .295535 Tw (such rule in Berry.) Tj /F2 10 Tf (Closure) Tj /F1 10 Tf ( is a technique that binds functions to ) Tj /F2 10 Tf (environments) Tj /F1 10 Tf (. The environment is a mapping that) Tj T* 0 Tw .425535 Tw (associates each free variable of a function with a value. In terms of implementation, closures associate the function) Tj T* 0 Tw .835256 Tw (prototype with its own variables. Function prototypes are generated at compile time, and environment is a runtime) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.180881 Tw (concept, so closures are also dynamically generated at runtime. Each closure binds the function prototype to the) Tj T* 0 Tw (environment when it is generated, for example, in the following example:) Tj T* ET
