BT 1 0 0 1 0 218 Tm 1.299243 Tw 12 TL /F1 10 Tf 0 0 0 rg (The virtual stack grows from left to right. When Berry code calls a native function, it will get an initial stack. The) Tj T* 0 Tw .479732 Tw (position of the first value of the stack is called ) Tj /F2 10 Tf (base) Tj /F1 10 Tf (, and the last position is called ) Tj /F2 10 Tf (top) Tj /F1 10 Tf (, the native function Only the) Tj T* 0 Tw .705186 Tw (value from the bottom of the stack to the position before the top of the stack can be accessed. The position of the) Tj T* 0 Tw -0.090236 Tw (bottom of the stack is fixed, while the position of the top of the stack can be moved, and the top of the stack is always) Tj T* 0 Tw .158766 Tw (empty. The reason for this property is that after the new value is pushed into the virtual stack, the original position of) Tj T* 0 Tw .314091 Tw (the top of the stack will be written The new value, the top pointer of the stack will move forward to the next position;) Tj T* 0 Tw .603221 Tw (conversely, if the value at the top of the virtual stack is popped, the top pointer of the stack will decrease 1. At this) Tj T* 0 Tw .872576 Tw (time, although the position of the top pointer of the stack is objectively Value, but this value is invalid and may be) Tj T* 0 Tw .241917 Tw (cleared at any time, so the pointer position on the top of the stack is still empty. When the virtual stack is empty, the) Tj T* 0 Tw .651205 Tw (bottom pointer ) Tj /F3 10 Tf (base) Tj /F1 10 Tf ( is equal to the top pointer ) Tj /F3 10 Tf (top) Tj /F1 10 Tf (. The virtual stack does not strictly follow the operating rules of) Tj T* 0 Tw .951147 Tw (the stack: in addition to push and pop, the virtual stack can also be accessed by index, and even insert or delete) Tj T* 0 Tw .529732 Tw (values at any position. There are two ways to index elements in the stack: one is based on the bottom of the stack) Tj T* 0 Tw .714004 Tw /F2 10 Tf (Absolute index) Tj /F1 10 Tf (, the absolute index value is a positive integer starting from 1; the other is based on the top of the) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.437584 Tw (stack ) Tj /F2 10 Tf (Relative index) Tj /F1 10 Tf (, The relative index value is a negative integer starting from ) Tj /F7 10 Tf 12 TL (-) Tj /F1 10 Tf 12 TL ( 1. Take Figure above as an) Tj T* 0 Tw .130671 Tw (example, the index value 1, 2\2058 is an absolute index, and the absolute index of an element is the distance from the) Tj T* 0 Tw .296164 Tw (element to the bottom of the stack. The index value ) Tj /F7 10 Tf 12 TL (-) Tj /F1 10 Tf 12 TL ( 1, ) Tj /F7 10 Tf 12 TL (-) Tj /F1 10 Tf 12 TL ( 2\205 ) Tj /F7 10 Tf 12 TL (-) Tj /F1 10 Tf 12 TL ( 8 is a relative index, and the relative index value of) Tj T* 0 Tw .119308 Tw (an element is the negative number of the distance from the element to the top of the stack. If an index value ) Tj /F4 10 Tf (index) Tj /F1 10 Tf ( is) Tj T* 0 Tw .276095 Tw (valid, then the element it refers to should be between the bottom of the stack and the top of the stack, which means) Tj T* 0 Tw (that the expression) Tj T* ET
